      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lawrence-women-in-love-57a193bdbfc9bf80\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":3,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"Birkin’s reiterated “I love you” directly avows his love, strongly supporting T. In conjunction with “Let love be enough then,” it also enacts a qualified acceptance of Ursula’s desired relational terms, supporting P. Its murmured, affective delivery does not independently establish E: the words are a deliberate, solicited response framed by concession, irony, and submission rather than a reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty is the strength of P: the utterance clearly participates in a relational concession, but Birkin’s irony and qualification make it less than an unequivocal pledge.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Birkin directly addresses Ursula with the first occurrence in the reiterated sequence “I love you then—I love you.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Ursula repeatedly asks Birkin to call her “my love” and to accept love as sufficient. Birkin has already avowed, “I love you right enough,” while insisting that he wants their relation to be “something else.” His eventual repetition is therefore sincere-looking as an avowal but also concessive and qualified: “then” and “I’m bored by the rest” mark his submission to Ursula’s formulation, while the narration explicitly mixes love with irony. This complexity affects T/P weighting but does not require E or O.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you right enough,” he said, grimly. “But I want it to be something else.”\",\"supports\":\"The prior explicit avowal strongly supports truth-conditional force while showing that Birkin distinguishes loving Ursula from accepting her conception of the relationship.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Let love be enough then. I love you then—I love you. I’m bored by the rest.”\",\"supports\":\"The repeated avowal supports T, while “Let love be enough then” gives it qualified performative force as acceptance of love as the relationship’s governing term.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He speaks while “murmuring in a subtle voice of love, and irony, and submission.”\",\"supports\":\"The narration supports mixed affect and a submissive concession, strengthening P while complicating sincerity and tone without establishing reflex-like E.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Ursula repeatedly solicits the words: “Say ‘my love’ to me, say it, say it.”\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is an elicited and contextually deliberate response, providing evidence against treating it as an involuntary expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit love utterance in Chapter XIII of D. H. Lawrence’s Women in Love.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified core occurrence and its grammatical presentation as a direct present avowal.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and P naturally capture the core force: an avowal of love combined with a qualified relational undertaking. Irony, solicitation, and emotional delivery are adequately represented as contextual qualifications, leaving no important residual core force.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
